@startuml cron-state
' CronStateMachine: the complete (state, event) -> state relation as implemented in
' CronStateMachine::transition. ExecutingTask is declared in CronState but is never
' entered: task execution is a synchronous side effect of the TaskDue transitions.
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/cron-state.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 2 in docs/util/cron/cron-manager.md
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam state {
  FontColor white
  FontStyle bold
  BorderColor #263238
  BackgroundColor #00838F
  AttributeFontColor #263238
  AttributeFontStyle plain
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #ECEFF1
  BorderColor #546E7A
}

legend top right
|= Concept |= Color |
| reachable state | <back:#00838F>        </back> |
| terminal state | <back:#C62828>        </back> |
| declared but unreachable | <back:#455A64>        </back> |
| task execution (side effect) | <back:#1565C0>        </back> |
endlegend

[*] --> CheckEvents : CronStateMachine::new

state "**CheckEvents**" as CheckEvents #00838F
CheckEvents : initial state — evaluate every event source
CheckEvents : try_recv() the task channel (non-blocking)
CheckEvents : peek() the min-heap for a due task
CheckEvents : load(Acquire) the termination flag

state "**DrainChannel**" as DrainChannel #00838F
DrainChannel : keep pulling until try_recv() reports Empty
DrainChannel : every received task is pushed onto the heap

state "**Sleeping**" as Sleeping #00838F
Sleeping : do_sleep() runs on entry to this state
Sleeping : sleeps min(Δt to next task, poll_interval_ms)

state "**ExecutingTask**" as ExecutingTask #455A64
ExecutingTask : declared in CronState — never entered

state "**Terminated**" as Terminated #C62828
Terminated : run() loop exits · the cron thread joins

CheckEvents --> DrainChannel : **TaskReceived**
DrainChannel --> DrainChannel : **TaskReceived**
DrainChannel --> CheckEvents : **ChannelDisconnected**\n(drain what remains)

CheckEvents -[#1565C0,bold]-> CheckEvents : **TaskDue** ⟳\nexecute_one_task()
DrainChannel -[#1565C0,bold]-> CheckEvents : **TaskDue**\nexecute_one_task()
Sleeping -[#1565C0,bold]-> CheckEvents : **TaskDue**\nexecute_one_task()

CheckEvents --> Sleeping : **NoEvents**
DrainChannel --> Sleeping : **NoEvents**\nchannel empty · nothing due
Sleeping --> CheckEvents : **TimerExpired**

CheckEvents -[#C62828,bold]-> Terminated : **ChannelDisconnected**\n∧ queue.is_empty()
CheckEvents -[#C62828,bold]-> Terminated : **TerminationRequested**
DrainChannel -[#C62828,bold]-> Terminated : **TerminationRequested**
Sleeping -[#C62828,bold]-> Terminated : **TerminationRequested**

ExecutingTask -[#455A64,dashed]-> CheckEvents : **TaskCompleted**\n{ success, should_requeue }\nvestigial arm — nothing\nconstructs this event

note bottom of ExecutingTask
  CronState::ExecutingTask and CronEvent::TaskCompleted exist,
  and transition() carries an arm for the pair, but no code path
  ever assigns state = ExecutingTask. execute_one_task() is called
  **inside** the TaskDue arms and returns before the next poll, so
  the reachable state set is
     { CheckEvents, DrainChannel, Sleeping, Terminated }.
  poll_event() encodes exactly this: its ExecutingTask branch is an
  unreachable!() with the message 'ExecutingTask polls internally'.
end note

note right of Terminated
  poll_event() tests the heap head **before** the
  termination flag, so a task that is already due
  still runs after request_shutdown(). A perpetually
  due task (interval_ms = 0, or a body slower than
  its own interval) therefore starves shutdown.
end note

note left of CheckEvents
  execute_one_task() pops the earliest task, runs it
  under catch_unwind, and re-queues it at
  now_ms() + interval only when the closure returned
  true **and** the metadata carries a recurrence
  interval. false ⇒ retire; panic ⇒ count and retire.
end note
@enduml
